1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,092
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,092 --> 00:00:08,696
Science, Unit 1: What Is a Computer? We'll
open up the box and see how a computer

3
00:00:08,696 --> 00:00:13,300
really works. After every lesson, we'll
work through a full example step by step,

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,256
and we'll finish by putting the whole unit
together.

5
00:00:17,429 --> 00:00:22,236
Phones, laptops, game consoles, even the
chip in a microwave. They look very

6
00:00:22,236 --> 00:00:22,869
different.

7
00:00:23,319 --> 00:00:25,879
But every computer does the same four
jobs.

8
00:00:26,329 --> 00:00:27,460
Let's see what they are.

9
00:00:28,610 --> 00:00:32,876
Job one is input. Input is information
going into the computer.

10
00:00:33,326 --> 00:00:38,229
A keyboard, a mouse, a touch screen, a
microphone and a camera are all input

11
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:38,745
devices.

12
00:00:39,895 --> 00:00:44,792
Job two is processing. The computer
follows instructions to change the input

13
00:00:44,792 --> 00:00:46,210
into something useful.

14
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:50,926
Adding numbers, sorting a list, or
brightening a photo are all processing.

15
00:00:52,076 --> 00:00:56,322
Job three is storage. The computer keeps
data so it can use it later.

16
00:00:56,772 --> 00:01:00,697
Your photos, your saved games, and the
apps themselves are all stored.

17
00:01:01,847 --> 00:01:06,263
Job four is output. Output is information
coming out of the computer.

18
00:01:06,713 --> 00:01:11,726
A screen, speakers, a printer, and a
phone's vibration are all output devices.

19
00:01:12,876 --> 00:01:16,844
Put it together, and you get the input,
process, store, output cycle.

20
00:01:17,294 --> 00:01:20,793
Take a calculator. You press seven, plus,
five. That's input.

21
00:01:21,243 --> 00:01:25,421
It adds them. That's processing. It
remembers the answer for the next step.

22
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:26,256
That's storage.

23
00:01:26,706 --> 00:01:28,626
And it shows twelve. That's output.

24
00:01:29,776 --> 00:01:33,894
Your turn. You take a photo with your
phone. Which job is the camera doing?

25
00:01:34,344 --> 00:01:35,538
Pause the video now.

26
00:01:35,988 --> 00:01:39,530
The camera is input. It sends light, as
data, into the phone.

27
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:42,966
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

28
00:01:44,117 --> 00:01:49,476
Let's apply input, processing, storage and
output to a machine you've probably used:

29
00:01:49,476 --> 00:01:51,008
a store's self-checkout.

30
00:01:52,158 --> 00:01:56,469
The problem: when you buy a snack at
self-checkout, which parts do each of the

31
00:01:56,469 --> 00:01:57,022
four jobs?

32
00:01:57,472 --> 00:02:01,568
We'll follow one purchase from start to
finish, and label every step.

33
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:05,811
Here's the purchase, step by step. You
scan the barcode.

34
00:02:06,261 --> 00:02:07,776
The machine looks up the price.

35
00:02:08,226 --> 00:02:11,639
It adds the price to your total, and keeps
a record of the sale.

36
00:02:12,089 --> 00:02:15,161
Then it shows the total on the screen and
prints a receipt.

37
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:21,303
Now label each step. Scanning the barcode
is input: data goes into the machine.

38
00:02:21,753 --> 00:02:25,038
Looking up the price and adding it to the
total are processing.

39
00:02:25,488 --> 00:02:28,923
Recording the sale is storage: the store
keeps it for later.

40
00:02:29,373 --> 00:02:32,168
Showing the total and printing the receipt
are output.

41
00:02:33,318 --> 00:02:37,414
Every job appears, in order: input,
processing, storage, output.

42
00:02:37,864 --> 00:02:42,849
A common mistake is calling the screen
input. A touch screen can be both: tapping

43
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:45,373
it is input, and what it shows is output.

44
00:02:46,523 --> 00:02:51,616
Your turn. A smart speaker hears you say,
play music, and starts a song. Which part

45
00:02:51,616 --> 00:02:53,456
is input, and which is output?

46
00:02:53,906 --> 00:02:55,101
Pause the video now.

47
00:02:55,551 --> 00:02:59,980
The microphone hearing your voice is
input. The speaker playing the song is

48
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:00,394
output.

49
00:03:00,844 --> 00:03:04,598
Next, we'll open up the box and see the
parts that do each job.

50
00:03:05,748 --> 00:03:09,439
If you open up a computer, you'll find
three parts that matter most.

51
00:03:09,889 --> 00:03:12,193
The C P U, the memory, and the storage.

52
00:03:13,343 --> 00:03:17,801
The C P U, or central processing unit, is
the part that processes.

53
00:03:18,251 --> 00:03:23,521
It follows instructions, one tiny step at
a time, billions of times every second.

54
00:03:23,971 --> 00:03:29,270
That speed is measured in gigahertz. Three
gigahertz means about three billion steps

55
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:29,837
a second.

56
00:03:30,987 --> 00:03:36,150
Memory, also called RAM, holds what the
computer is working on right now.

57
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:40,333
The app you have open, and the web page
you're reading, sit in memory.

58
00:03:40,783 --> 00:03:44,666
Memory is very fast, so the C P U never
has to wait long.

59
00:03:45,816 --> 00:03:49,187
Storage holds everything else, even when
the power is off.

60
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:52,709
That's your files, your photos, and all
your installed apps.

61
00:03:53,159 --> 00:03:58,641
Storage is a hard drive or a solid state
drive, and it holds much more than memory.

62
00:03:59,791 --> 00:04:03,077
Here's a way to picture it. Think of a
student doing homework.

63
00:04:03,527 --> 00:04:05,959
The student is the C P U, doing the work.

64
00:04:06,409 --> 00:04:10,078
The desk is memory: small, but everything
on it is right at hand.

65
00:04:10,528 --> 00:04:14,795
The backpack is storage: it holds much
more, but it's slower to reach into.

66
00:04:15,945 --> 00:04:19,017
All three are connected on a board called
the motherboard.

67
00:04:19,467 --> 00:04:24,821
Data moves between them along tiny wires,
again and again, as you use the computer.

68
00:04:25,971 --> 00:04:30,914
Your turn. You open a document you saved
last week. Where was it while the computer

69
00:04:30,914 --> 00:04:31,390
was off?

70
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:33,035
Pause the video now.

71
00:04:33,485 --> 00:04:38,678
It was in storage. When you open it, a
copy moves into memory so the C P U can

72
00:04:38,678 --> 00:04:39,543
work with it.

73
00:04:39,993 --> 00:04:42,980
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

74
00:04:44,130 --> 00:04:48,781
Let's follow a photo through the CPU,
memory and storage, as you edit it.

75
00:04:49,931 --> 00:04:53,813
The problem: you open a saved photo,
brighten it, and save it again.

76
00:04:54,263 --> 00:04:57,762
At each step, where is the photo, and
which part is working on it?

77
00:04:58,912 --> 00:05:01,579
Here are the steps. The photo starts on
the drive.

78
00:05:02,029 --> 00:05:03,949
Opening it copies it into memory.

79
00:05:04,399 --> 00:05:07,193
The CPU changes each pixel to brighten it.

80
00:05:07,643 --> 00:05:10,353
Saving copies the new version back to
storage.

81
00:05:11,503 --> 00:05:15,385
Let's track it. Before you open it, the
photo is only in storage.

82
00:05:15,835 --> 00:05:19,889
After opening, there are two copies: one
in storage, one in memory.

83
00:05:20,339 --> 00:05:24,243
While brightening, the CPU reads and
writes the copy in memory.

84
00:05:24,693 --> 00:05:27,530
After saving, storage holds the new
version too.

85
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,688
The CPU only ever works on data in memory.

86
00:05:32,138 --> 00:05:37,660
Common mistake: thinking the CPU edits the
file on the drive directly. It doesn't.

87
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,882
Data is copied into memory first.

88
00:05:41,032 --> 00:05:45,663
Your turn. You edit a document, and the
power goes out before you save. Which

89
00:05:45,663 --> 00:05:46,685
version survives?

90
00:05:47,135 --> 00:05:48,330
Pause the video now.

91
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:53,239
Only the old version in storage. The edits
were in memory, and they were lost.

92
00:05:53,689 --> 00:05:55,971
Next, how the CPU runs instructions.

93
00:05:57,121 --> 00:06:02,646
A program is a list of instructions. But
how does the C P U actually follow them?

94
00:06:03,096 --> 00:06:05,955
It repeats a simple three step loop, over
and over.

95
00:06:07,105 --> 00:06:11,500
Step one is fetch. The C P U gets the next
instruction from memory.

96
00:06:11,950 --> 00:06:16,984
A small counter inside the C P U keeps
track of which instruction comes next.

97
00:06:18,134 --> 00:06:22,444
Step two is decode. The C P U works out
what the instruction means.

98
00:06:22,894 --> 00:06:26,328
Is it asking to add? To compare? To copy a
number somewhere?

99
00:06:27,478 --> 00:06:31,446
Step three is execute. The C P U carries
out the instruction.

100
00:06:31,896 --> 00:06:36,014
Then the counter moves on, and the loop
starts again with the next instruction.

101
00:06:37,164 --> 00:06:41,302
Let's watch a tiny program. Load five. Add
three. Store the result.

102
00:06:41,752 --> 00:06:47,777
Fetch: load five. Decode: it's a load.
Execute: five goes into a register, a tiny

103
00:06:47,777 --> 00:06:50,008
storage spot inside the C P U.

104
00:06:50,458 --> 00:06:55,130
Fetch: add three. Decode: it's an add.
Execute: the register now holds eight.

105
00:06:55,580 --> 00:07:00,210
Fetch: store. Decode: it's a store.
Execute: eight is copied back to memory.

106
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:06,800
Each loop is called a cycle. A modern C P
U does billions of cycles a second.

107
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:12,776
Many C P Us also have several cores. Each
core runs its own fetch, decode, execute

108
00:07:12,776 --> 00:07:16,146
loop, so they can work on different tasks
at once.

109
00:07:17,296 --> 00:07:20,389
Your turn. Put these in order: execute,
fetch, decode.

110
00:07:20,839 --> 00:07:22,034
Pause the video now.

111
00:07:22,484 --> 00:07:25,641
The order is fetch, decode, execute. Then
it repeats.

112
00:07:26,091 --> 00:07:29,078
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

113
00:07:30,228 --> 00:07:34,752
Let's trace a short program through the
fetch, decode, execute cycle, one

114
00:07:34,752 --> 00:07:36,116
instruction at a time.

115
00:07:37,266 --> 00:07:42,253
The problem: the CPU runs four
instructions. What is in register A, and

116
00:07:42,253 --> 00:07:44,711
in memory spot X, when it finishes?

117
00:07:45,861 --> 00:07:48,805
Here's the program. Load seven into
register A.

118
00:07:49,255 --> 00:07:50,343
Add five to A.

119
00:07:50,793 --> 00:07:52,052
Subtract two from A.

120
00:07:52,502 --> 00:07:54,123
Store A into memory spot X.

121
00:07:55,273 --> 00:08:01,235
Each row is one cycle. Cycle one: fetch
LOAD 7, decode it as a load, execute: A

122
00:08:01,235 --> 00:08:02,292
becomes seven.

123
00:08:02,742 --> 00:08:05,238
Cycle two: ADD 5. A becomes twelve.

124
00:08:05,688 --> 00:08:08,099
Cycle three: SUB 2. A becomes ten.

125
00:08:08,549 --> 00:08:13,434
Cycle four: STORE X. X becomes ten. The
counter now points past the end.

126
00:08:14,584 --> 00:08:17,421
When the program ends, A is ten and X is
ten.

127
00:08:17,871 --> 00:08:23,529
Common mistake: changing X too early. Only
STORE writes to memory. Until then, the

128
00:08:23,529 --> 00:08:25,530
result stays in the register.

129
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:32,611
Your turn. LOAD 4, ADD 4, ADD 4, STORE Y.
What is Y, and how many cycles run?

130
00:08:33,061 --> 00:08:34,255
Pause the video now.

131
00:08:34,705 --> 00:08:36,668
Y is twelve, after four cycles.

132
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:40,403
Next, why memory forgets everything when
the power goes off.

133
00:08:41,553 --> 00:08:46,149
You're writing an essay, and the power
goes out. When the computer comes back,

134
00:08:46,149 --> 00:08:47,740
your last changes are gone.

135
00:08:48,190 --> 00:08:51,112
But the files you saved yesterday are
fine. Why?

136
00:08:52,262 --> 00:08:56,870
Memory, or RAM, is volatile. That means it
needs power to keep its data.

137
00:08:57,320 --> 00:09:03,078
RAM stores each bit as a tiny electric
charge. When the power stops, the charges

138
00:09:03,078 --> 00:09:05,022
fade, and the data is gone.

139
00:09:06,172 --> 00:09:10,204
Storage is non volatile. It keeps data
with no power at all.

140
00:09:10,654 --> 00:09:16,188
A solid state drive traps charge in cells
that hold it for years. A hard drive

141
00:09:16,188 --> 00:09:20,019
stores bits as tiny magnetic spots on a
spinning disk.

142
00:09:21,169 --> 00:09:23,900
So why not use storage for everything?
Speed.

143
00:09:24,350 --> 00:09:29,923
RAM can be about a hundred times faster
than a solid state drive, and thousands of

144
00:09:29,923 --> 00:09:32,030
times faster than a hard drive.

145
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:38,369
And why not use RAM for everything? Cost.
RAM costs much more per gigabyte, and it

146
00:09:38,369 --> 00:09:38,944
forgets.

147
00:09:40,094 --> 00:09:41,779
That's why computers use both.

148
00:09:42,229 --> 00:09:47,392
A typical laptop might have sixteen
gigabytes of RAM, and five hundred twelve

149
00:09:47,392 --> 00:09:48,800
gigabytes of storage.

150
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:54,583
Programs and files live in storage, and
get copied into RAM while you use them.

151
00:09:55,733 --> 00:09:57,589
This is also why saving matters.

152
00:09:58,039 --> 00:10:03,066
When you press save, the computer copies
your work from RAM to storage, where it's

153
00:10:03,066 --> 00:10:03,372
safe.

154
00:10:04,522 --> 00:10:08,277
Your turn. Which one is volatile: RAM, or
a solid state drive?

155
00:10:08,727 --> 00:10:09,922
Pause the video now.

156
00:10:10,372 --> 00:10:14,340
RAM is volatile. It loses its data when
the power is off.

157
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:17,776
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

158
00:10:18,927 --> 00:10:22,618
Let's do the math on RAM and storage for a
real laptop.

159
00:10:23,768 --> 00:10:30,274
The problem: a laptop has 16 gigabytes of
RAM and 512 gigabytes of storage.

160
00:10:30,724 --> 00:10:37,025
The system uses 4 gigabytes of RAM. You
open a browser, a video editor and a game.

161
00:10:37,025 --> 00:10:39,407
Do they all fit in RAM at once?

162
00:10:40,557 --> 00:10:44,013
Here's the plan. Start with the RAM in use
by the system.

163
00:10:44,463 --> 00:10:45,850
Add each app as it opens.

164
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:49,777
Compare the running total with the 16
gigabytes available.

165
00:10:50,927 --> 00:10:53,402
System: four gigabytes used, twelve free.

166
00:10:53,852 --> 00:10:55,708
Browser: seven used, nine free.

167
00:10:56,158 --> 00:10:58,440
Video editor: thirteen used, three free.

168
00:10:58,890 --> 00:11:03,477
Game: eighteen needed, but only sixteen
exist. Two gigabytes don't fit.

169
00:11:04,627 --> 00:11:08,126
They don't all fit. The computer runs
short by two gigabytes.

170
00:11:08,576 --> 00:11:14,051
The operating system can move some data
out to storage temporarily, but storage is

171
00:11:14,051 --> 00:11:19,394
much slower, so everything slows down.
That's why more RAM makes a computer feel

172
00:11:19,394 --> 00:11:19,861
faster.

173
00:11:21,011 --> 00:11:26,540
Your turn. The same laptop runs only the
system, the browser and the game. Do they

174
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:28,563
fit, and how much RAM is free?

175
00:11:29,013 --> 00:11:30,208
Pause the video now.

176
00:11:30,658 --> 00:11:33,324
Yes. They use twelve gigabytes, so four
are free.

177
00:11:33,774 --> 00:11:37,550
Next, the program that runs all the
others: the operating system.

178
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:42,668
When you turn on a computer, one program
starts before all the others.

179
00:11:43,118 --> 00:11:48,755
It's the operating system. Windows, mac O
S, Linux, Android and i O S are all

180
00:11:48,755 --> 00:11:50,073
operating systems.

181
00:11:51,223 --> 00:11:54,551
The operating system sits between your
apps and the hardware.

182
00:11:55,001 --> 00:12:00,577
Apps don't talk to the C P U, memory, or
storage directly. They ask the operating

183
00:12:00,577 --> 00:12:01,059
system.

184
00:12:02,209 --> 00:12:04,300
Job one: managing the C P U.

185
00:12:04,750 --> 00:12:10,142
You might have music, a browser and a game
running at once. The operating system

186
00:12:10,142 --> 00:12:15,400
switches the C P U between them, so fast
that they all seem to run at the same

187
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:15,737
time.

188
00:12:16,887 --> 00:12:18,508
Job two: managing memory.

189
00:12:18,958 --> 00:12:24,042
The operating system gives each app its
own space in RAM, and keeps apps from

190
00:12:24,042 --> 00:12:25,891
reading each other's memory.

191
00:12:27,041 --> 00:12:29,537
Job three: managing files and devices.

192
00:12:29,987 --> 00:12:34,790
It organizes storage into files and
folders, and it talks to the keyboard, the

193
00:12:34,790 --> 00:12:37,006
screen, the printer and the network.

194
00:12:38,156 --> 00:12:40,759
Job four: security and the user interface.

195
00:12:41,209 --> 00:12:46,056
It handles logins and permissions, like
whether an app may use your camera. And it

196
00:12:46,056 --> 00:12:49,721
draws the desktop, the windows and the
home screen you tap on.

197
00:12:50,871 --> 00:12:56,261
Your turn. Two apps are open, and only one
C P U core. Which part of the computer

198
00:12:56,261 --> 00:12:58,124
decides which app runs next?

199
00:12:58,574 --> 00:12:59,769
Pause the video now.

200
00:13:00,219 --> 00:13:04,101
The operating system. It schedules the C P
U between apps.

201
00:13:04,551 --> 00:13:07,538
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

202
00:13:08,688 --> 00:13:13,000
Let's see exactly how an operating system
shares one CPU core between three apps.

203
00:13:15,150 --> 00:13:18,905
The problem: music, a browser and a game
all run on one core.

204
00:13:19,355 --> 00:13:24,543
The operating system gives each app a turn
of ten milliseconds, in order. Who runs

205
00:13:24,543 --> 00:13:26,821
during the first sixty milliseconds?

206
00:13:27,971 --> 00:13:32,302
Here's the rule the scheduler follows.
Keep a list of the apps that are ready.

207
00:13:32,752 --> 00:13:34,992
Give the first app a ten millisecond turn.

208
00:13:35,442 --> 00:13:39,261
When its time is up, move it to the back
of the line, and run the next app.

209
00:13:40,411 --> 00:13:43,333
Zero to ten: music runs. Then it goes to
the back.

210
00:13:43,783 --> 00:13:46,621
Ten to twenty: browser. Twenty to thirty:
game.

211
00:13:47,071 --> 00:13:52,105
Thirty to forty: music again. Forty to
fifty: browser. Fifty to sixty: game.

212
00:13:53,255 --> 00:13:58,665
Each app ran twice in sixty milliseconds.
That's so fast it feels like they all run

213
00:13:58,665 --> 00:13:59,186
at once.

214
00:13:59,636 --> 00:14:04,336
Common mistake: thinking the apps really
run at the same time on one core. They

215
00:14:04,336 --> 00:14:04,991
take turns.

216
00:14:06,141 --> 00:14:10,350
Your turn. With the same three apps, which
app runs from seventy to eighty

217
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:11,090
milliseconds?

218
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:12,735
Pause the video now.

219
00:14:13,185 --> 00:14:18,027
The browser. Sixty to seventy is music,
then seventy to eighty is the browser.

220
00:14:18,477 --> 00:14:21,421
Next, what software is, and how programs
get made.

221
00:14:22,571 --> 00:14:25,323
Hardware is the parts of a computer you
can touch.

222
00:14:25,773 --> 00:14:29,101
Software is the instructions that tell the
hardware what to do.

223
00:14:30,251 --> 00:14:32,128
There are two main kinds of software.

224
00:14:32,578 --> 00:14:37,648
System software runs the computer itself,
like the operating system and device

225
00:14:37,648 --> 00:14:38,168
drivers.

226
00:14:38,618 --> 00:14:43,681
Application software, or apps, does jobs
for you: a browser, a game, a word

227
00:14:43,681 --> 00:14:44,356
processor.

228
00:14:45,506 --> 00:14:50,714
Every program starts as source code: text
written by a person in a programming

229
00:14:50,714 --> 00:14:52,717
language, like Python or Java.

230
00:14:53,167 --> 00:14:56,815
Here's one line of Python. It tells the
computer to print hello.

231
00:14:57,965 --> 00:15:03,208
But the C P U only understands machine
code: instructions written as binary

232
00:15:03,208 --> 00:15:03,768
numbers.

233
00:15:04,218 --> 00:15:06,372
So source code has to be translated.

234
00:15:07,522 --> 00:15:09,122
There are two ways to translate.

235
00:15:09,572 --> 00:15:15,283
A compiler translates the whole program
into machine code before it runs. C and C

236
00:15:15,283 --> 00:15:16,975
plus plus work this way.

237
00:15:17,425 --> 00:15:22,879
An interpreter translates and runs the
program one line at a time. Python usually

238
00:15:22,879 --> 00:15:23,889
works this way.

239
00:15:25,039 --> 00:15:30,229
Real programs are built step by step.
Plan, write the code, test it, and fix the

240
00:15:30,229 --> 00:15:31,396
bugs. Then repeat.

241
00:15:31,846 --> 00:15:36,499
That cycle is how every app you use was
made, and it's how you'll write programs

242
00:15:36,499 --> 00:15:36,732
too.

243
00:15:37,882 --> 00:15:42,191
Your turn. Is a web browser system
software, or application software?

244
00:15:42,641 --> 00:15:43,836
Pause the video now.

245
00:15:44,286 --> 00:15:47,166
It's application software. It does a job
for you.

246
00:15:47,616 --> 00:15:50,602
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

247
00:15:51,753 --> 00:15:55,920
Let's see the difference between a
compiler and an interpreter on a program

248
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:56,532
with a bug.

249
00:15:57,682 --> 00:16:01,991
The problem: this three-line Python
program has a typo on line three.

250
00:16:02,441 --> 00:16:06,132
What happens when an interpreter runs it?
And what would a compiler do?

251
00:16:07,282 --> 00:16:09,607
Here's the program. Line one prints hello.

252
00:16:10,057 --> 00:16:11,849
Line two prints two plus three.

253
00:16:12,299 --> 00:16:15,691
Line three has a typo: p r n t instead of
print.

254
00:16:16,841 --> 00:16:20,297
With Python's interpreter, line one runs
and prints hello.

255
00:16:20,747 --> 00:16:22,539
Line two runs and prints five.

256
00:16:22,989 --> 00:16:26,360
Line three fails with a NameError, and the
program stops.

257
00:16:26,810 --> 00:16:31,349
A compiler checks the whole program first,
so it would report the error before

258
00:16:31,349 --> 00:16:32,164
anything runs.

259
00:16:33,314 --> 00:16:37,954
The interpreter ran two lines before
stopping. A compiler would run nothing

260
00:16:37,954 --> 00:16:39,501
until the error is fixed.

261
00:16:39,951 --> 00:16:45,347
Either way, the fix is the same: correct
the typo, and test again. That's the plan,

262
00:16:45,347 --> 00:16:46,842
write, test, fix cycle.

263
00:16:47,992 --> 00:16:52,600
Your turn. If the typo were on line one
instead, what would the interpreter print?

264
00:16:53,050 --> 00:16:54,244
Pause the video now.

265
00:16:54,694 --> 00:16:57,084
Nothing. It stops with an error on line
one.

266
00:16:57,534 --> 00:16:59,518
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

267
00:17:00,667 --> 00:17:03,376
You've learned each part of a computer on
its own.

268
00:17:03,826 --> 00:17:08,625
Now let's follow one real action through
all of them: typing a letter into a

269
00:17:08,625 --> 00:17:10,141
document, and saving it.

270
00:17:11,291 --> 00:17:14,512
Here's the plan. Each step uses one idea
from the unit.

271
00:17:14,962 --> 00:17:19,357
Input, the operating system, the CPU,
memory, output, and storage.

272
00:17:20,507 --> 00:17:25,941
Step one: input. You press A. The keyboard
sends a small code, which is data, to the

273
00:17:25,941 --> 00:17:26,523
computer.

274
00:17:27,673 --> 00:17:30,510
Step two: the operating system receives
the code.

275
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:35,708
It checks which app is active, the text
editor, and delivers the letter to it.

276
00:17:35,708 --> 00:17:38,022
Apps never read the keyboard directly.

277
00:17:39,172 --> 00:17:41,902
Step three: the CPU runs the editor's
code.

278
00:17:42,352 --> 00:17:47,566
It fetches each instruction, decodes it,
and executes it, adding the letter to the

279
00:17:47,566 --> 00:17:52,208
document. That takes thousands of cycles,
in a tiny fraction of a second.

280
00:17:53,358 --> 00:17:58,884
Step four: the document being edited is in
RAM, so the new letter goes into memory.

281
00:17:59,334 --> 00:18:03,046
Then the screen is redrawn, and the letter
appears. That's output.

282
00:18:04,196 --> 00:18:09,910
Step five: you press save. The operating
system copies the document from RAM to

283
00:18:09,910 --> 00:18:10,489
storage.

284
00:18:10,939 --> 00:18:13,179
Now it's safe, even if the power goes off.

285
00:18:14,329 --> 00:18:19,024
Your turn. The power goes out after step
four, before you save. Is the letter A

286
00:18:19,024 --> 00:18:19,321
kept?

287
00:18:19,771 --> 00:18:20,966
Pause the video now.

288
00:18:21,416 --> 00:18:25,682
No. It was only in memory. The saved file
on the drive doesn't have it.

289
00:18:26,132 --> 00:18:31,444
That's the end of Unit 1. Next, Unit 2:
how computers store everything as numbers.

290
00:18:32,594 --> 00:18:37,023
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

291
00:18:37,023 --> 00:18:37,416
lesson.

292
00:18:37,866 --> 00:18:38,698
See you there.

